A block's custom properties are named like classes: the module's
four-character prefix, then three characters of the variable's seed. The
seed is the module, the block's interpolated declarations with their
selectors and at-rules, the path, and the type. The same
path read with the same type in several rules of the block (`c` in the
base rule, in `&:hover` and in `@media`) is one variable. `other` reads
`c` too, but its interpolated declarations differ, so it has its own
variable.

  $ cat > input.ml <<EOF
  > let card c w = [%css {|
  >   color: \$(c);
  >   width: \$(w);
  >   &:hover { border-color: \$(c); }
  >   @media (min-width: 600px) { background-color: \$(c); }
  > |}]
  > let other c = [%css "color: \$(c)"]
  > EOF
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl input.ml -o output.ml
  $ refmt --parse ml --print re output.ml
  [@css "@property --ivpcjnw{syntax:\"*\";inherits:false;}"];
  [@css "@property --ivpclrh{syntax:\"*\";inherits:false;}"];
  [@css "@property --ivpcpc4{syntax:\"*\";inherits:false;}"];
  [@css
    ".css-ivpctp1{color:var(--ivpcjnw);width:var(--ivpclrh);}.css-ivpctp1:hover{border-color:var(--ivpcjnw);}@media (min-width: 600px) {.css-ivpctp1{background-color:var(--ivpcjnw);}}"
  ];
  [@css ".css-ivpchao{color:var(--ivpcpc4);}"];
  [@css.classes
    [
      ("css-ivpctp1", 3, "Input.card", "input.ml", 2, 2, 6, 0),
      ("css-ivpchao", 4, "Input.other", "input.ml", 7, 21, 7, 32),
    ]
  ];
  [@css.bindings [("Input.card", ""), ("Input.other", "")]];
  let card = (c, w) =>
    CSS.make(
      "css-ivpctp1",
      [
        ("--ivpcjnw", CSS.Types.Color.toString(c)),
        ("--ivpclrh", CSS.Types.Width.toString(w)),
      ],
    );
  let other = c =>
    CSS.make("css-ivpchao", [("--ivpcpc4", CSS.Types.Color.toString(c))]);
  $ styled-ppx.generate output.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @property --ivpcjnw{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  @property --ivpclrh{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  @property --ivpcpc4{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  .css-ivpctp1{color:var(--ivpcjnw);width:var(--ivpclrh);}.css-ivpctp1:hover{border-color:var(--ivpcjnw);}@media (min-width: 600px) {.css-ivpctp1{background-color:var(--ivpcjnw);}}
  .css-ivpchao{color:var(--ivpcpc4);}

Two blocks of one module share a variable when their interpolations have
the same seed: the same interpolated declarations under the same selectors
and at-rules, the same path, the same type. `slide` and `track` both set
`transform: translateX($(offset))` (the static `transition` of `track`
is not part of the seed), so `both` sets one variable twice and the
later operand wins. `Other.slide` is the same block in another module: its
variable has another name.

  $ cat > carousel.ml <<EOF
  > let slide offset = [%css "transform: translateX(\$(offset))"]
  > let track offset = [%css {|transform: translateX(\$(offset)); transition: transform 0.3s;|}]
  > let both a b = CSS.merge (slide a) (track b)
  > EOF
  $ cat > other.ml <<EOF
  > let slide offset = [%css "transform: translateX(\$(offset))"]
  > EOF
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl carousel.ml -o carousel_out.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl other.ml -o other_out.ml
  $ grep -oh -- '("--[0-9a-z_-]*", (CSS' carousel_out.ml other_out.ml
  ("--0b6fn62", (CSS
  ("--0b6fn62", (CSS
  ("--sjjcvfe", (CSS
  $ styled-ppx.generate carousel_out.ml other_out.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @property --0b6fn62{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  .css-0b6fl3n{-webkit-transform:translateX(var(--0b6fn62));-moz-transform:translateX(var(--0b6fn62));-ms-transform:translateX(var(--0b6fn62));transform:translateX(var(--0b6fn62));}
  .css-0b6fijk{-webkit-transform:translateX(var(--0b6fn62));-moz-transform:translateX(var(--0b6fn62));-ms-transform:translateX(var(--0b6fn62));transform:translateX(var(--0b6fn62));transition:transform 0.3s;}
  @property --sjjcvfe{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  .css-sjjcb0q{-webkit-transform:translateX(var(--sjjcvfe));-moz-transform:translateX(var(--sjjcvfe));-ms-transform:translateX(var(--sjjcvfe));transform:translateX(var(--sjjcvfe));}

Three characters leave room for two different variables of one module to
get the same name. The later one widens its suffix, as a class does, and
the earlier one keeps its name. `$(c92)` and `$(c169)` get the same three
characters in `Input`:

  $ mkdir wide
  $ cat > wide/input.ml <<EOF
  > let a c92 = [%css "color: \$(c92)"]
  > let b c169 = [%css "color: \$(c169)"]
  > EOF
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl wide/input.ml -o wide/output.ml
  $ grep -o -- '"--[0-9a-z]*", (CSS.Types.Color.toString c[0-9]*' wide/output.ml
  "--ivpcltk", (CSS.Types.Color.toString c92
  "--ivpchltk", (CSS.Types.Color.toString c169

Both names end in the same three characters, the clash that made the
second one widen:

  $ grep -o -- '"--[0-9a-z]*", (CSS' wide/output.ml | cut -d'"' -f2 | awk '{ s = substr($0, length($0) - 2); n[s]++ } END { for (s in n) print s, n[s] }'
  ltk 2
